{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "non_recommendation_outcome (Recommended = no)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "Avg Subcategory Score",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Aggregates the service subcategory ratings (seat, staff, food, IFE, value). These subjective quality scores plausibly drive the reviewer's recommendation, so this is a candidate cause/driver of the outcome, not a common cause of some other treatment. It is a predictor being attributed, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "Value For Money Poor",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Binary flag derived from the Value For Money rating. It is a candidate cause whose effect on non-recommendation is being attributed. It is not a common cause of any other treatment and the outcome, so it is a treatment/driver, not a confounder."
    },
    {
      "name": "Overall Rating Band",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Overall Rating is a summary satisfaction score that sits downstream of the subcategory scores and is essentially co-determined with (a near-proxy of) the recommendation decision. On the path subcategory-scores -> overall-rating -> recommendation it is a mediator/outcome proxy; adjusting for it would bias, not enable, causal attribution."
    },
    {
      "name": "Weakest Category",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Identifies which subcategory scored lowest, i.e. names the candidate driver responsible for a poor review. It is a descriptor of the candidate cause, not a common cause of both a treatment and the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "Non-Rec Risk Factors",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "A post-hoc engineered concatenation of 'poor_X' flags. It mixes candidate causes (poor_value_for_money, poor_seat_comfort, etc.) with 'very_low_overall_rating', which is an outcome proxy/mediator. It is a circular attribution label constructed from outcome-adjacent variables rather than an adjustable confounder."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["Avg Subcategory Score", "Value For Money Poor", "Weakest Category"],
  "confounders": [],
  "confounder_quality": 0.0,
  "rationale": "The added columns perform a descriptive/correlational decomposition of the outcome: they flag which service subcategory scores are poor and label the weakest driver. This surfaces candidate causes/treatments (poor value-for-money, weakest category, per-category poor flags) but supplies ZERO confounders. A confounder here would be a common cause of both a subcategory rating and the recommendation that must be adjusted to avoid biased attribution -- e.g. Type of Traveller, Route, Month Flown, or reviewer disposition. None of these are added; nothing is surfaced from the data/text as a common cause. Worse, several columns (Overall Rating Band, and 'very_low_overall_rating' inside Non-Rec Risk Factors) are downstream proxies/mediators of the recommendation itself; treating them as explanatory factors would be a modelling mistake. Because the required treatment+confounder structure is absent (no common causes to adjust for), the causal-estimation claim is not realised. confounder_quality = 0."
}

